// Annex E.3.4 / §9.17.3 $limit with the drain-to-source limiter, which Table E.2
// lists as taking NO arguments beyond the access function — which is why this
// call has two arguments where limit_pnj.va has four.
//
// THE NAME. The string below is Table E.2's, `vdslim`, because this fixture
// cites Table E.2 and a suite whose premise is portability may not write a
// tool's private spelling. SPICE3 and VerA both call the function `limvds`
// (DEVlimvds), so on VerA the string reaches no built-in — which costs the
// fixture nothing, because §9.17.3 says "if the string refers to an unknown or
// unsupported function, the simulator is responsible for determining the
// appropriate limiting algorithm, just as if no string had been supplied". Both
// spellings are conforming and neither is diagnosable, so there is no reject to
// demand and no xfail to write. The claim below is name-independent: it holds
// for a tool that recognises `vdslim`, for one that recognises only `limvds`,
// and for one that substitutes its own limiter under the escape clause.
//
// WHAT IS PINNED. §9.17.3: "When the simulator has converged, the return value
// of the $limit() function is the value of the access function reference, within
// appropriate tolerances." At the answer the limiter is transparent and the
// stamped drain current is the one V(mid) implies.
//
// WHAT THE HARNESS SUPPLIES, and why the digit still holds. §9.17.3 also says
// "in all cases, the simulator is responsible for determining if limiting should
// be applied and what the return value is on a given iteration", and this
// harness does not iterate: `//! bias` imposes x and the device is evaluated
// ONCE, so the convergence sentence is not handed to the fixture for free. The
// digit has to survive the worst single iteration instead, and 0.4 V does for
// every cold start a SPICE-family tool uses. DEVlimvds(vnew, vold) with any
// stored vold below 3.5 V clamps only at +4 V rising and -0.5 V falling, and
// 0.4 V is inside both; only a previous iterate at or above 3.5 V would answer
// max(0.4, 2) = 2, and there is no previous iterate here — a channel is
// well-conditioned at 0 V, which is where this limiter is left cold.
//
// WHY THE PROBE IS AN INTERNAL NET. A limiter corrects an unknown the device
// owns. Probing the module PORT instead makes the correction a write to a node
// the host imposes, and a simulator is entitled to decline it — VerA does,
// silently — so a port-probing form of this fixture would put the transparency
// question to a call the tool had already refused to limit. The bias directive
// holds `mid` at 0.4 V, and the branch to `p` is written in terms of the LIMITED
// value, so a clamp that fired would move the stamped current and not only the
// assertion.
//
// TOLERANCE. §9.17.3 fixes only "within appropriate tolerances". The only number
// the LRM supplies for a Voltage-natured quantity is Annex D disciplines.vams
// `nature Voltage ... abstol = 1e-6`. 1e-12 is the CURRENT abstol. 1e-6 still
// discriminates: the only clamp reachable here moves the answer by 1.6 V.
//
//! lrm E.3.4
//! lrm 9.17.3
//! bias V(p) = 0.4, V(mid) = 0.4
`include "check.vh"
module annex_e_vdslim(p);
  inout p; electrical p; electrical mid; real limited;
  analog begin
    limited = $limit(V(mid), "vdslim");
    I(mid, p) <+ (limited - V(p)) / 1.0;
    // 1e-6 is the Annex D `nature Voltage` abstol.
    `CHECK("vdslim returns the value of its access function", limited, 0.4, 1e-6);
  end
endmodule
